NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON LINCOLN BENEFIT LIFE V. AEI LIFE
Judge Ambro’s concurring opinion (joined by the other two judges on the panel) pleaded with the Supreme Court to apply a functional approach to the citizenship of unincorporated associations. In closing, it noted that there were “some cracks in Carden’s wall—circuit courts are divided over how to determine the citizenship of [investment] trusts.” The best-known form of investment trust is the real estate investment trust or REIT. An investor can purchase and sell ownership shares in a REIT, which generates income through investments in real property. Investors in REITs may be called “members” or “shareholders.”

In Americold Realty Trust v. ConAgra Foods, Inc., 577 U.S. ___, 136 S. Ct. 1012 (2016), the Supreme Court faced the question of whether a REIT should be treated as a corporation for diversity jurisdiction purposes. The Supreme Court held in a unanimous opinion that a REIT is a citizen of every state in which its members, including shareholders, are citizens. What considerations might lead some judges to embrace the current rule for citizenship of unincorporated associations, notwithstanding the criticism the rule receives? One factor might be the effect of the rule on the caseload of the federal courts. What effect would the adoption of Judge Ambro’s concurrence have on the federal courts’ docket?
